u002Fauth\u002Fme"," answers ",[272,1441,1442],{},"204"," while the page is signed in, and ",[272,1445,1446],{},"POST \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Flogout"," signs it out; both take ",[272,1449,1450],{},"credentials: 'include'"," as well.",[247,1453,1455],{"id":1454},"how-oidc-works-with-your-application","How OIDC Works with Your Application",[1457,1458,1459,1462,1465,1468],"ol",{},[255,1460,1461],{},"Your platform administrator enables one or more OIDC configurations for your application (e.g., Google, Microsoft)",[255,1463,1464],{},"Your UI looks the user up by email at its API host; for a user of an identity provider the answer carries the provider's authorization URL, and the UI sends the browser there",[255,1466,1467],{},"The provider returns the browser to the API host, which validates the provider's response, looks the user up in your application, and sets the session cookie",[255,1469,1470,1471,1474,1475,1478,1479,1483,1484,1487],{},"The browser returns to ",[272,1472,1473],{},"\u002F"," on the site that started the sign-in, which connects with the session; a sign-in that fails returns instead to the ",[272,1476,1477],{},"\u002Flogin"," page of your application's ",[277,1480,1482],{"href":1481},"\u002Fapps\u002Fapplication-structure\u002Fapplications-and-projects#applications","primary UI"," with an ",[272,1485,1486],{},"error"," parameter",[247,1489,1491],{"id":1490},"reusable-providers","Reusable Providers",[243,1493,1494,1495,1497,1498,1501],{},"Kinotic OS registers once with providers like Google and Microsoft. The platform operator provisions these as ",[272,1496,274],{}," entities; your organization admin enables them for your application by adding the configuration id to your application's ",[272,1499,1500],{},"oidcConfigurationIds",". End users see \"Kinotic OS\" on the consent screen, and your application benefits without any provider registration.",[243,1503,1504,1505,1507,1508,1510],{},"The same ",[272,1506,274],{}," shape is used for an organization's enterprise SSO (e.g. a corporate Okta instance) — see ",[277,1509,167],{"href":168}," for how the platform-vs-org distinction is captured by reference rather than by a flag on the entity.",[239,1512,1514],{"id":1513},"user-management","User Management",[243,1516,1517,1518,1521,1522,1525],{},"Application-scope users are ",[258,1519,1520],{},"pre-created"," by an administrator before they can authenticate. This is a deliberate security design — having a valid Google account does not automatically grant access to your application. Self-service signup applies to the ",[277,1523,1524],{"href":163},"Organization scope"," (the admin who runs the org); the same data model supports application-scope self-signup, but enabling it is admin-controlled.",[243,1527,1528,1529,1532],{},"Your organization administrator manages your application's users from the organization's ",[258,1530,1531],{},"Members"," page:",[252,1534,1535,1541],{},[255,1536,1537,1540],{},[258,1538,1539],{},"Local users"," are created with an email and password",[255,1542,1543,1546],{},[258,1544,1545],{},"OIDC users"," are created with an email only — the OIDC subject is linked automatically on first login",[243,1548,1549,1550,1552],{},"For details on user and OIDC configuration management, see the ",[277,1551,162],{"href":163}," platform guide.",[1554,1555,1556],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .s7zQu, html code.shiki .s7zQu{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-light-font-style:italic;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-default-font-style:italic;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic}html pre.shiki code .sMK4o, html code.shiki .sMK4o{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF}html pre.shiki code .sTEyZ, html code.shiki .sTEyZ{--shiki-light:#90A4AE;--shiki-default:#EEFFFF;--shiki-dark:#BABED8}html pre.shiki code .sfazB, html code.shiki .sfazB{--shiki-light:#91B859;--shiki-default:#C3E88D;--shiki-dark:#C3E88D}html pre.shiki code .s2Zo4, html code.shiki .s2Zo4{--shiki-light:#6182B8;--sh